codemap 8
path panel-tree
kind flow
author ai
group gui
note How the window is divided and how the reader rearranges it. Below the top bar and above the status bar, the window is one tree of panels: a split holds two branches side by side or one above the other at a ratio, and a leaf panel holds a stack of views shown as tabs. The ten views (Paths, Symbols, Files, Path, Diff, Graph, Listing, Results, Xrefs, Output) each live in at most one panel. The tree is Panels in the Model, laid out top-down every frame from the body's rectangle. The reader drags a sash to resize, drags a tab onto a panel to join it or onto a panel's edge to split it, splits a panel with its | and - buttons, closes it with x, and fills a panel from the picker behind +, a searchable list of views. Navigation still speaks in centre tabs: whatever switches the tab brings that view to front wherever it lives, and adds it back next to the last one shown if it was in no panel. Pointer gestures are read before the tree is built and applied at once, so the frame that sees a drag already draws it. The tree is saved to the user's layout file whenever it changes and no drag is held, and a launch starts from that file (the io-layout path), or from the default tree when there is none.

